---
import AppCTA from '../../components/AppCTA.astro';
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import RelatedContent from '../../components/RelatedContent.astro';
import VocabTable from '../../components/VocabTable.astro';
import { wordsByMarathi } from '../../lib/learn';
import { siteUrl } from '../../lib/site';

const thisWords = wordsByMarathi([
	'हा', 'ही', 'हे', 'इथे', 'तिथे', 'असा', 'तसा',
]);
---

<Learn
	title="हा, ही, हे — Saying “This” in Marathi Correctly"
	description="Marathi has three words for this: हा (masculine), ही (feminine), हे (neuter). The simple gender rule, plus इथे/तिथे and असा/तसा."
	kicker="Word meaning"
	crumbs={[
		{ label: 'Blog', href: siteUrl('/blog/') },
		{ label: 'हा ही हे — this in Marathi', href: siteUrl('/blog/this-in-marathi/') },
	]}
	canonicalPath="/blog/this-in-marathi/"
	about="ha hi he in marathi; this in marathi"
	meta={['3 genders', '7 pointer words']}
	faq={[
		{
			question: 'How do you know which of हा, ही, हे to use?',
			answer: 'Match the noun, not the speaker: हा with masculine nouns, ही with feminine, हे with neuter. Every word table on this site carries the noun with its correct form, so checking a word once teaches you both at once.',
		},
		{
			question: 'What is the difference between इथे and तिथे?',
			answer: 'इथे = here (near the speaker), तिथे = there (away from the speaker). The same near/far split gives असा (like this) and तसा (like that).',
		},
	]}
>
	<h2>The short answer</h2>
	<p class="lede">
		Marathi splits "this" by gender:
		<span class="mr" lang="mr">हा</span> for masculine nouns,
		<span class="mr" lang="mr">ही</span> for feminine,
		<span class="mr" lang="mr">हे</span> for neuter. Same word, three
		uniforms — the noun decides which one it wears.
	</p>

	<h2>The pointer words</h2>
	<p>
		These seven are the navigation system of Marathi sentences: the three
		"this" forms, the here/there pair (<span class="mr" lang="mr">इथे</span>
		/ <span class="mr" lang="mr">तिथे</span>) and the like-this/like-that
		pair (<span class="mr" lang="mr">असा</span> /
		<span class="mr" lang="mr">तसा</span>).
	</p>
	<VocabTable words={thisWords} caption="This, here and like-this" />

	<h2>The pattern that repeats everywhere</h2>
	<p>
		Once the हा/ही/हे habit clicks, relatives fall in line for free:
		<span class="mr" lang="mr">माझा</span>/<span class="mr" lang="mr">माझी</span>
		(my, masculine/feminine) follow the same agreement, and so do almost
		all adjectives. If you are coming from Hindi, this feels familiar —
		the feminine differs from Hindi in places, though, so trust the
		gendered tables over guesswork.
	</p>

	<AppCTA variant="grammar" context="blog-this-in-marathi" />

	<RelatedContent
		links={[
			{ label: 'My/your: माझा vs माझी', href: siteUrl('/grammar/possessives/') },
			{ label: 'Grammar notes, all topics', href: siteUrl('/grammar/') },
			{ label: 'Beginner words', href: siteUrl('/vocabulary/beginners/') },
		]}
	/>
</Learn>
